In this lesson, we'll look at non-monic quadratics, which are equations where the x-squared term has a coefficient other than one, like two x-squared, or five x-squared, and so on. Don't worry, there are two solid methods to crack these. First up is the split the middle, or ac method. You take the first coefficient times the last coefficient, then hunt for two numbers that multiply to that product and add up to the middle coefficient. Once you have them, you split the middle term into those two parts, then factor by grouping. That just means finding a common factor in each pair, then pulling out the shared bracket. Sound tricky? It gets easier with practice. The second method is the cross-product, or box method. You're basically guessing four numbers that multiply across and down to give you the first and last coefficients, while the diagonals add up to the middle one. You trial and tweak until everything lines up. For tricky non-monics, grouping usually scales better, especially when those numbers aren't small and friendly. Now let's see it in action.